---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'CAT Quantitative Aptitude: Three Arithmetic Topics That Pay Off | StudyRoadmap';
const description = 'A practical look at the CAT Quantitative Aptitude section, drawn from the Percentages, Profit-Loss, and Time-Work notes on StudyRoadmap.';
const publishDate = '2026-09-17';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <Navbar />

  <main id="main-content" class="min-h-screen bg-surface-50 dark:bg-surface-900">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <p class="text-sm text-brand-600 dark:text-brand-400 font-semibold mb-2">CAT Strategy · Quant</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white leading-tight mb-3">
          CAT Quantitative Aptitude: Three Arithmetic Topics That Pay Off
        </h1>
        <p class="text-surface-600 dark:text-surface-400 text-lg">
          CAT QA has shifted toward applied arithmetic, not pure algebra. Here are the three arithmetic topics that most consistently deliver marks, drawn from the CAT notes on StudyRoadmap.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-surface-500 mt-3">Published {publishDate}</p>
      </header>

      <section class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
        <p>
          The CAT Quant section is no longer a Graduate Record Exam in disguise. Since 2020, IIMs have leaned toward word problems grounded in commerce, finance, and everyday reasoning. Three arithmetic chapters carry most of this load, and getting them right is worth more than memorising obscure algebra identities.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">1. Percentages — the universal translator</h2>
        <p>
          Almost every other arithmetic topic is percentages in disguise. Profit/loss percentages, ratio comparisons, and growth-rate problems all reduce to a percentage change. The <a href="/notes/cat/qa/qa-001/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Percentages</a> note on StudyRoadmap covers successive percentage change, percentage points vs percent-of-percent, and base-year pitfalls. Make this chapter your default mental model: if a problem isn't obviously about something else, write it as a percentage.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">2. Profit and loss — the modern CAT favourite</h2>
        <p>
          Modern CAT questions frame profit/loss in business scenarios — discount chains, GST-inclusive pricing, marked price vs selling price, and break-even analysis. The <a href="/notes/cat/qa/qa-002/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Profit-Loss</a> note gives you the formula ladder: SP = CP × (1 + profit%), then applies it to successive discount problems. Train on the variants rather than the basic formula.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">3. Time and work — including pipes and cisterns</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/notes/cat/qa/qa-003/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Time-Work</a> chapter looks elementary until CAT wraps it in worker-efficiency problems, alternate-day work patterns, and combined-pipeline scenarios. The two habits that pay off: (a) always convert to a unit rate first ("one worker completes 1/x of the work per day"), and (b) use LCM of the time units to avoid fractions.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">What this means for your plan</h2>
        <p>
          The StudyRoadmap <a href="/exams/cat/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">CAT exam hub</a> builds the auto-plan around these three chapters first. If you have two months, expect percentages in week one, profit/loss and time-work in weeks two and three. The remaining arithmetic topics — ratio, time-distance, equations — build on top of these three.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-surface-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Practising the modern CAT style</h2>
        <p>
          CAT 2024 and 2025 papers leaned heavily on applied arithmetic with multi-step word problems. Solve 15–20 word problems per arithmetic topic per week, and time yourself: the goal is under 2 minutes per question once the topic is mastered. The free <a href="/roadmap/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">AI roadmap generator</a> on StudyRoadmap adjusts to your hours per day and locks these chapters in early.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
